  "attributionNote": "Revealed by Bahá’u’lláh in Baghdad in Persian, and translated by Shoghi Effendi; the Reference Library publishes it in the collection The Call of the Divine Beloved.",
  "description": "The Seven Valleys is a Sufi treatise in the form of an answer to a question: the seeker passes through search, love, knowledge, unity, contentment, wonder, and true poverty and annihilation, and at each stage Bahá’u’lláh describes what is seen and how the language of the mystics is to be understood. The earlier Sufi poets and their stories are quoted throughout, and the treatise ends where the mystics end — in what cannot be told.",
  "historicalContext": "Revealed in Baghdad while the new teaching was being given shape after the Báb, the Seven Valleys is the principal mystical work of the Bahá’í writings, read both as a statement of the Sufi path and as the Bahá’í account of how that path leads further than the stage at which the older teachers stopped.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete: the whole treatise as the Bahá’í Reference Library publishes it in The Call of the Divine Beloved, streamed from bahai.org at read time. The companion treatise revealed with it, The Four Valleys, is a separate record in this library; the other tablets in that collection are not imported.",
